```json
{
  "mammals": {
    "domestic": ["dog", "cow", "cat"],
    "wild": ["wolf"]
  },
  "birds": {
    "domestic": [],
    "wild": ["eagle", "owl"]
  },
  "fish": {
    "domestic": [],
    "wild": ["salmon", "trout"]
  },
  "amphibians": {
    "domestic": [],
    "wild": ["frog", "toad"]
  }
}
```

Ten animals, four classes. Two honest notes on the scheme: every bird, fish and amphibian on your list is wild, so those domestic arrays are empty rather than omitted, which keeps the structure honest about what the list contains. And 'domestic' is doing quiet work here: a dog is a domesticated species, while a wolf is its wild relative, so the split inside mammals is really about the species' relationship to humans, not about individual animals.

```json
{
  "sessions": [
    {"day": "Monday", "start": "19:00", "end": "20:30", "subject": "maths"},
    {"day": "Wednesday", "start": "19:00", "end": "20:30", "subject": "biology"},
    {"day": "Friday", "start": "19:00", "end": "19:45", "subject": "maths"},
    {"day": "Friday", "start": "19:45", "end": "20:30", "subject": "French"}
  ],
  "weekly_totals_minutes": {"maths": 135, "biology": 90, "French": 45}
}
```

How the constraints were satisfied: maths gets its two slots on Monday and Friday, biology takes Wednesday, and French shares Friday, split at 19:45 so no evening repeats a subject. One consequence worth seeing plainly: French ends up with half the time of biology and a third of maths, because your availability has only three evenings and maths claimed two of the four slots. If French matters more than that, the constraint that has to move is maths down to one slot, and the JSON makes that trade visible instead of hiding it.

```json
{
  "book": {
    "section": {"type": "enum", "values": ["Fiction", "Non-fiction", "Children", "Reference"]},
    "shelf_code": {"type": "string", "pattern": "SECTION-ROW", "example": "FIC-03"},
    "reading_level": {"type": "enum", "values": ["Early", "Middle", "Advanced"]}
  },
  "rules": [
    {"id": "R1", "when": {"section": "Reference"}, "then": {"loanable": false},
     "note": "Reference books never leave the building"},
    {"id": "R2", "when": {"section": "Children"}, "then": {"shelf_height": "low"},
     "note": "Children's books are always on low shelves"},
    {"id": "R3", "when": {"section": "Children"}, "then": {"reading_level_default": "Early"},
     "note": "Default only; a Children's book may still be marked Middle"}
  ]
}
```

Design choices worth explaining. The two hard requirements became rules R1 and R2, stated as machine-checkable conditions rather than prose, so whoever writes the cataloguing tool can enforce them automatically: a Reference book simply cannot be issued a loan. R3 is deliberately a default, not a rule, because your requirements did not say children's books are always Early level, and turning a tendency into a constraint would misfile every older child's book. The shelf code embeds the section, FIC-03, so a book found on the floor announces where it belongs without consulting the catalogue.

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "required": ["title", "author"],
  "properties": {
    "title": {"type": "string", "description": "Full title of the book"},
    "author": {"type": "string", "description": "Author's name as printed on the cover"},
    "year_published": {"type": "integer", "description": "Year this edition was published"},
    "pages": {"type": "integer", "minimum": 1, "description": "Page count of this edition"},
    "genres": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "One or more genre labels"},
    "finished": {"type": "boolean", "default": false, "description": "Whether you have read it to the end"}
  }
}
```

Only title and author are required, because a record you cannot name is useless, while every other field can be filled in later; making everything required is the classic schema mistake that leads to junk values like page counts of 0 entered just to satisfy the form. Pages carries a minimum of 1 for the same reason. Note that year_published describes the edition, not the work: a 2020 reprint of an 1860 novel has year_published 2020 in this schema, and if the group ever cares about first publication, that deserves its own separate field rather than an argument about which year 'counts'.
